<TopicPage slug="subjects/design/intermediate/prototyping-and-wireframes">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Designing is a conversation with an idea, and both halves of this page — drawing and
    modelling — are how the conversation happens. You already know from the UX research
    lesson that real users, not opinions, settle design questions; this lesson is about the
    cheaper half of that discipline: <strong>representing an idea before you commit to
    building it</strong>, so that the expensive questions get asked early. A sketch is an idea
    made inspectable; a prototype is an idea made testable. Both are deliberately
    <em>disposable</em> — the whole skill lies in spending the least material, minutes and
    polish needed to answer the question at hand. This page covers the designer's drawing
    vocabulary (freehand, orthographic, isometric, exploded and CAD), then the modelling
    ladder from quick exploratory models to full prototypes, with the fidelity logic that
    tells you which to build and when.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="fidelity as budget, wireframes as arguments, risk-first, tools" widget="equationStep" config={protoCfg} />

  <h2>Freehand sketching: the fastest design language</h2>
  <p>
    Freehand sketching is design thinking made visible, and its speed is the whole point.
    A pencil line can be changed before the ink of the thought is dry — which is why
    professionals sketch in <strong>thin, light construction lines</strong> first (proportions,
    major divisions) and only darken the confident lines on top. Good design sketches use
    the same conventions again and again: <strong>line weight</strong> to show importance
    (a heavy outline around the object, lighter lines for detail), simple
    <strong>annotation</strong> in short notes ("grip zone", "thumb clearance — 25 mm?") and
    <strong>arrows</strong> for forces, movement or the direction a part slides. Sketches are
    also social instruments: in a team meeting, drawing two alternatives side by side on
    the same sheet turns a vague argument into a concrete comparison, and because neither
    drawing looks finished, nobody defends one out of pride. The rule worth internalising:
    <em>quantity beats quality early</em> — six rough variations teach you more than one
    polished picture, because the variations force you to decide what actually matters.
  </p>

  <h2>Orthographic projection: the exact language of making</h2>
  <p>
    When a design must be built to size, freehand charm is not enough; the drawing must
    carry <strong>true dimensions</strong>. Orthographic projection solves this by showing the
    object as a set of flat views, each looking straight down one axis: the <strong>front
    elevation</strong>, the <strong>side elevation</strong> (or end view) and the <strong>plan</strong>
    (looking down from above). Because each view looks squarely at the object, every edge
    parallel to the view appears at its true length — no perspective, no foreshortening —
    so a maker can measure, or read a written dimension, straight off the page. The views
    align with each other: edges that share a height line up horizontally between front and
    side; edges that share a depth line up between plan and side. Conventions do the rest:
    visible edges are solid lines, hidden edges are dashed, centre-lines (of holes and
    axes of symmetry) are long-dash-short-dash, and dimensions carry extension lines and
    arrowheads with the number placed clear of the geometry.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>A worked example.</strong> Suppose you are designing a simple phone stand: a
    wedge, 100 mm tall at the back, leaning at 15°, with a 10 mm lip at the front to stop
    the phone sliding. The front view is a rectangle 100 mm wide — you decide the width
    from the widest phone you support, say 85 mm, plus 7.5 mm lip on each side, so the
    front view shows 85 + 7.5 + 7.5 = 100 mm. The side view carries the real information:
    a right-triangle profile with the 100 mm back edge, the base running forward, the
    10 mm lip rising at the front, and the leaning face drawn at its true 15° from
    vertical. From that side view you can compute what the maker needs: the base depth is
    100 ÷ tan(15°) ≈ 373 mm — obviously too deep, so you re-lean at 30° and get
    100 ÷ tan(30°) ≈ 173 mm, still deep, and settle on a two-part folding stand whose
    base is 90 mm and whose lean is set by a hinge strut rather than the wedge. One
    sketch on graph paper, five minutes of trigonometry, and a bad proportion was caught
    before any wood was cut — which is precisely what the drawing is for.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> orthographic views are "the boring flat drawings". In
  fact they are the only views that tell the truth about size. An isometric sketch of a
  circle on a tilted face shows an ellipse on wrong axes; an orthographic side view shows
  the same circle as a straight line and the front view shows it at true shape. Flatness
  is not a limitation — it is the measurement system.</p>

  <h2>Isometric, exploded and other pictorial views</h2>
  <p>
    Orthographic views measure well but are hard for non-designers to read — clients see
    three flat shapes, not a product. <strong>Isometric drawing</strong> bridges the gap: the
    object is drawn with its three axes set 120° apart on the page, which puts the two
    horizontal axes at 30° above the horizontal. The name means "equal measure": along
    each of the three axes you can draw directly at scale, so a 60 mm edge is drawn as
    60 mm (at whatever scale you chose) on any axis. The compromise is that circles and
    angles on tilted faces distort — a round hole becomes an ellipse — so isometric
    drawings explain while orthographic drawings specify. <strong>Exploded views</strong>
    complete the family: the assembly is drawn with its parts separated slightly along
    their assembly axes, each still aligned with where it belongs. Flat-pack furniture
    instructions and engine manuals are exploded views doing their job — showing not just
    what the parts are but <em>how they come together and in what order</em>. A good exploded
    view answers assembly questions before the workshop asks them: which fastener goes
    where, what the stack-up order is, which face is hidden after assembly.
  </p>

  <h2>CAD: the drawing becomes a model</h2>
  <p>
    Computer-aided design (CAD) does not replace the hand skills — professionals still
    sketch first — but it changes what a drawing <em>is</em>. In <strong>2D CAD</strong> you draw
    precise orthographic views with the software enforcing accuracy: snap grids, exact
    dimensions, automatic line types. In <strong>3D parametric CAD</strong> you build the object
    as a model of features — sketch a profile, extrude it 12 mm, fillet the edges, shell
    it hollow — and the crucial word is <em>parametric</em>: dimensions are stored as
    editable values, not fixed ink. Change the phone-stand width from 85 mm to 95 mm and
    the model regenerates, the holes move with the faces they are attached to, and the
    drawing views update themselves. That is the deep difference from paper: a CAD model
    is a <em>plan for change</em>. From the same model you can produce the orthographic
    drawing set automatically, render photorealistic images for clients, check
    interference between moving parts, and export files for 3D printing or CNC
    machining — the design and the manufacturing instructions share one source of truth.
  </p>

  <h2>Exploratory models: rough questions in cheap material</h2>
  <p>
    The syllabus draws a line between <strong>exploratory models</strong> and
    <strong>prototypes</strong>, and the distinction is about the question being asked.
    An exploratory model is quick, rough and thrown together to investigate one aspect of
    a concept: does this shape sit comfortably in the hand? Does that joint look right in
    proportion? Card, foam board, modelling clay, wire and tape are the standard kit
    because they are fast to shape and fast to discard. A classic school-project sequence:
    for a handheld garden tool, foam handles cut in three different diameters (28, 34 and
    42 mm) get taped to a stick and held for a minute each; the grip that stops
    fatiguing wins, and nothing else about the tool existed yet. Exploratory models
    deliberately avoid finish — sanding and painting them would be a waste, and worse, it
    would make them precious. The moment a model becomes precious, the team stops
    questioning it, and a model nobody questions teaches nothing.
  </p>

  <h2>Prototypes: the idea made testable</h2>
  <p>
    A prototype, by contrast, is a model built to <strong>behave like the product</strong> in
    the specific way you want to test. It can still be rough, but it must function for the
    question at hand: a working mechanism cut from plywood to test whether a cam follower
    actually lifts the lid; a paper smartphone screenset carried by a "computer" behind
    the screen who swaps sheets as the user taps (the <strong>Wizard of Oz</strong> trick); a
    full-size card dummy of a workstation to check reach and sight-lines. The
    professional discipline is to <strong>prototype the risk, not the product</strong>: list
    what could kill this design — the mechanism jams, the flow confuses users, the
    material sags — and build the smallest thing that tests the scariest item first. For
    digital products the same logic gives the <strong>foldable fidelity ladder</strong>:
    paper screens (minutes, flow and wording), wireframes (grey boxes, structure and
    hierarchy — the grids lesson's arguments in skeleton form), clickable prototypes
    (hours, sequence and transitions), and high-fidelity builds (days, visual detail and
    real data). Each rung costs roughly an order of magnitude more than the one below,
    so you climb only when the question below has been answered.
  </p>
  <p><em>Common misconception:</em> a prototype is a nearly finished product, so teams
  postpone prototyping until the design is "far enough along". This inverts the value.
  The whole economic point of a prototype is that it is cheap <em>while the design is
  still changeable</em>; a flaw found in a paper prototype costs an afternoon, the same
  flaw found after tooling or launch costs a recall. Prototype early, prototype ugly,
  and let the polish arrive as the answers accumulate.</p>

  <h2>Choosing the representation: a decision procedure</h2>
  <p>
    With the full vocabulary available, choosing becomes a short checklist. First,
    <strong>write the question</strong>: not "test the design" but "does the lid mechanism
    clear the hinge boss?" or "can users find the search box?". Second, pick the
    <strong>cheapest representation that can answer that question</strong> — a question about
    proportion is a sketch; about reach is a card model; about sequence is a wireframe
    flow; about strength is the actual material. Third, <strong>declare what is fake</strong>:
    every prototype lies somewhere (the card has no weight, the Wizard has no server), so
    state the lie out loud to avoid conclusions the representation cannot support. Fourth,
    <strong>plan the discard</strong>: a prototype that cannot be thrown away will be defended
    instead of learned from. Run the loop — question, build, test, discard or refine —
    and the design converges the way the design-and-make projects always have: through
    many small, cheap, honest failures.
  </p>

  <h2>Going deeper</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>First vs third angle projection.</strong> Two conventions exist for arranging
    orthographic views. In <strong>first angle</strong> (common in Europe), the view from the
    left is drawn on the <em>right</em> of the front view — the object is imagined pressed
    into the planes between viewer and paper. In <strong>third angle</strong> (US standard),
    the left view lands on the left — the planes are imagined between object and viewer,
    like a glass box unfolding. Drawings declare their convention with the truncated-cone
    symbol; mixing them up produces mirrored parts.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Horizontal and vertical prototypes.</strong> Digital teams inherit a useful
    vocabulary from software engineering: a <em>horizontal</em> prototype skins the entire
    journey shallowly (every screen, none functional) and answers breadth questions about
    flow; a <em>vertical</em> prototype builds one path fully (search works end-to-end with
    real data) and answers depth questions about feasibility. Mature projects alternate —
    horizontal first to fix the skeleton, vertical slices to de-risk the organs — which
    is the same risk-first logic this page applied to card models.
  </p>
  <p>
    <strong>Where the field is moving.</strong> Generative CAD tools now propose geometry
    from constraints (topology optimisation produces the bone-like brackets Airbus
    prints for the A320 family), and AI layout tools draft wireframes from a text brief.
    The representation skills on this page survive every one of these shifts, because
    they are about the <em>questions</em>: someone still has to know which view answers a
    measurement question, which fidelity answers a flow question, and when the model in
    hand has said all it can say. Tools change what a line costs; they do not change
    what questions are worth asking first.
  </p>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Buxton, Sketching User Experiences; the entrepreneurship lesson's MVP logic; the research lesson's usability loop; Cambridge IGCSE Design &amp; Technology 0445 syllabus, section 1.3.</p>

